{%- set churchIntro -%}<p class="page-intro">St Egwin's is the parish church of Norton, on the road between the Lenches and Evesham. It is named after St Egwin, the bishop of Worcester who founded Evesham Abbey in the early 700s. The parish formally belongs to the Evesham benefice but shares the ARCH Sunday rota. The church is listed Grade II*.</p>{%- endset -%}
{%- set churchAbout -%}<h2>A short history</h2>
    <p>The chancel dates from the 14th century and the west tower, with its battlements, corner pinnacles and gargoyles, from the 15th. The nave was largely rebuilt when the church was restored in 1844. The north transept keeps its 14th-century roof.</p>
    <h2>What to look out for</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The 12th-century marble lectern, which came from Evesham Abbey. It was found buried in the abbey churchyard in 1813 and set up here in 1865.</li>
      <li>Three tombs of the Bigge family in the north transept: Thomas Bigge (died 1581) and his wife lying side by side; Sir Thomas Bigge (died 1613) and his wife kneeling under an arch; and Sir Thomas Bigge (died 1621), in alabaster under a canopy on black marble columns. A helmet, gauntlets and spurs hang above them.</li>
      <li>The 15th-century carved font, the 17th-century pulpit and the linenfold panelling in the chancel.</li>
    </ul>{%- endset -%}
